<div class="box key">
  <span class="lbl">Central claim</span>
  <p>For a specified equilibrium binding mechanism, an impossible pair of reaction orders rules out the proposed molecular response. An attainable pair can occur at some positive equilibrium. Connecting two modules also requires checking their absolute rates and how those rates respond to the shared pool.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="s1" class="essay-section"><span class="n">1 · Problem</span> When do two submodels describe the same interface?</h2>
<p>In their 2012 whole-cell model of <i>Mycoplasma genitalium</i>, Karr and colleagues represented 28 cellular processes through 16 categories of shared state. Each process could use mathematics suited to its data. Metabolism used flux-balance analysis, a way to constrain reaction flows, while RNA and protein degradation used stochastic models. At each one-second step the algorithm allocated shared resources, ran the process models on their allocations, and combined their updates. This schedule assumed that the submodels could execute separately over a short step.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-karr">1</a></sup></p>

