<p>Now suppose one module supplies a molecular pool and another removes from it. If both read the same state, their rates can be evaluated in one simulation. If that state is also meant to be stationary, the two rates must agree. A separate question is whether either module's response to a change in molecular abundance can arise from the mechanism it claims to represent. Which part of this compatibility problem can be tested before the coupled simulation runs?</p>

<h2 id="s2" class="essay-section"><span class="n">2 · Challenge</span> Connecting model elements leaves scientific checks to do</h2>
<p>Macklin, Ruggero, and Covert distinguished linking common state variables from making the combined behavior satisfy physical and biological constraints.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-macklin">2</a></sup> Karr's energy budget illustrates why absolute quantities matter. The modeled processes accounted for 44.3% of the energy experimentally observed to be produced during growth.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-karr">1</a></sup> Locating the remainder requires investigating which processes consume energy and how much each consumes. Freddolino and Tavazoie treated discrepancies of this kind as leads for refining the model.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-freddolino">3</a></sup></p>

<p>Standards and mathematical frameworks address different parts of this problem. The Systems Biology Markup Language (SBML) encodes a model. Its hierarchical composition package specifies how to nest submodels, reference ports, and replace model elements. SED-ML records the simulation protocol.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-sbml">4</a>, <a href="#r-comp">5</a>, <a href="#r-sedml">6</a></sup> In a formal framework for open reaction networks, Baez and Pollard compose steady-state relations that retain both concentrations and boundary flows.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-baez">7</a></sup> The screen developed below uses only two slopes of one output at equilibrium. Its narrower input is useful precisely because a contradiction can be detected even when absolute rates are unavailable.</p>

