<p>The distinction matters in practice. Choi and Covert found that separating tRNA charging, which supplies amino acid carriers, from translation, which uses them, could deplete the charged pool within a simulation step. Their analysis cites observed charging rates of 1.8–8.1 cycles per tRNA molecule per second and typical model steps of 1–2 seconds. In their integrated <i>E. coli</i> model, the catalytic constants estimated to support growth were 7.6-fold higher, at the median, than the highest curated in vitro measurements for the corresponding enzymes.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-choi">8</a></sup> The first result concerns the timing of exchange between processes. The second concerns absolute capacity. The example motivates checking both when two processes share a pool.</p>

<h2 id="s3" class="essay-section"><span class="n">3 · Approach</span> One binding pair gives an exact feasibility region</h2>
<p>Consider reversible binding <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo>⇌</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">C</mi></mrow></math>. The variables <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> are free concentrations and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> is the concentration of the bound complex. At equilibrium they obey <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is a dissociation constant with concentration units. The conserved totals are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Each intervention changes one total and holds the other total and affinity fixed. Holding free substrate fixed would define a different intervention.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-lecture">9</a></sup></p>

<p>Take the readout to be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, with a fixed <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. When <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is a flux, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> has inverse-time units and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> has concentration per time units. Here this is a readout of an equilibrium state. It introduces no additional binding dynamics. The two <em>reaction orders</em> describe fractional changes in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> per fractional change in one input. For example, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow></math> means that near the reference state, a 1% increase in total enzyme gives about a 0.7% increase in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∂</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow></math><span class="tag">(1)</span></div>

<p>Since <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, differentiating the equilibrium relation gives <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The constant <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> drops out of the logarithmic derivatives. Multiplying the ordinary derivatives by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, respectively, gives the expressions below. The first numerator simplifies because <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>. The second follows by exchanging <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow></math><span class="tag">(2)</span></div>

<p>At every finite positive state, each order is less than one because its numerator omits one positive term in the denominator. Their sum exceeds one by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>. The orders therefore obey the joint condition</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1.</mn></mrow></math><span class="tag">(3)</span></div>

<p>The converse makes this an exact description of the attainable set. For a pair satisfying (3), choose any <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and construct the free concentrations as follows.</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mfrac><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mfrac><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow></math><span class="tag">(4)</span></div>

<p>Both concentrations are positive. Set <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>S</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub></mrow></math> and recover the two totals by conservation. Substitution into (2) gives the original orders. Thus every interior point of the triangle is attainable for some positive totals. The vertices <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> and the edges represent limits. If the totals and affinity are already specified, their equilibrium gives a single predicted order pair to check. Lecture 11 derives this region and shows how changing the readout from complex to free enzyme changes the constraints.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-lecture">9</a></sup></p>

<h2 id="s4" class="essay-section"><span class="n">4 · Proof</span> Four tests separate attainable orders from flux balance</h2>

<h3>Tests A and B · One binding mechanism, two screening outcomes</h3>
<p>The conservation equations give a quadratic for <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></math>, its physical root is</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow></math><span class="tag">(5)</span></div>

<p>In Test A1, both totals and affinity equal one model concentration unit. Equation (5) gives <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.381966</mn></mrow></math>, and (2) gives <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.723607</mn></mrow></math>. To check the derivatives numerically, the code perturbs each total by factors <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> and divides the change in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. At <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the largest absolute difference from (2) across the four cases is about <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mn>3.3</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Swapping the totals in A2 and A3 swaps the orders, as the symmetry of (2) predicts. In A2, almost all substrate is bound. More enzyme therefore changes the complex little, while more substrate increases the complex nearly in proportion.</p>

