<h3>Tests C and D · Equal orders leave the absolute scale free</h3>
<p>Hold the two totals and affinity at the A1 values. Let two modules share the same binding response <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> and supply and remove a distinct pool <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, respectively. Their rates are <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Here the rates refer to the same pool concentration, with fixed volume and matched units. With these two contributions, the material balance is</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow></math><span class="tag">(6)</span></div>

<p>For Test C, set <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> in inverse model time units. Because both constants disappear from (1), the two modules have identical orders, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.723607</mn><mo separator="true">,</mo><mn>0.723607</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>. Their rates are approximately <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mn>0.381966</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mn>1.145898</mn></mrow></math> concentration units per time. The net rate is approximately <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.763932</mn></mrow></math>, so the designated state fails the stationarity requirement. For Test D, setting <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">o</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">u</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> makes the rates equal and the net rate zero at the same state. The order pair stays the same. In general, multiplication by any positive constant preserves the entire local order pair while changing the rate scale.</p>

