<h2 id="s5" class="essay-section"><span class="n">5 · Significance</span> Stability needs the response to the shared pool</h2>
<p>Keep Test D's reference rates and introduce feedback from <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> be the target concentration, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math>. At the A1 binding state, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> is the positive rate in Test D. As a minimal extension, let inflow be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and outflow be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. For either exponent, both rates equal <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></math>. When the binding totals are perturbed at fixed <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, both rates also retain the same orders from (2). This lets us vary the shared-pool feedback while holding the first two checks fixed.</p>

<p>The linearized growth rate of a small disturbance in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is the derivative of the net rate at <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></math>. It is</p>

<div class="eq"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence="false" stretchy="true" minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>γ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="true" minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">]</mo><mo fence="true">∣</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow></math><span class="tag">(7)</span></div>

<p>For <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>0.190983</mn></mrow></math> per time unit. A small increase in <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> grows because the inflow increases faster than the outflow. For <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.190983</mn></mrow></math> per time unit and a small increase decays. The same binding orders and the same balanced rates are therefore compatible with opposite local stability outcomes. The scalar example illustrates the turnover-scaled balance of response orders developed in Lecture 11.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-lecture">9</a></sup></p>

<p>For a proposed interface, the first check is to identify the molecular readout and independently vary each total at fixed affinity. An order pair outside the region rejects the specified mechanism. At known totals, comparison with (2) supplies a more specific prediction. When the slopes are estimated from data, the rejection should account for their joint uncertainty. The next check compares signed flux contributions in the same pool and units at the intended operating point. If that state balances, the dynamics check needs the rate derivatives with respect to all coupled pools. Structural kinetic modeling illustrates this step by combining local rate sensitivities with scaled steady-state fluxes and stoichiometry in a Jacobian.<sup class="c"><a href="#r-steuer">10</a></sup> A test of the binding-equilibrium approximation itself would require comparing those dynamics with a model that retains finite binding rates.</p>

<p>The gain is a specific early rejection criterion for an interface that claims this binding mechanism. It turns an apparent fit at one state into a testable prediction about how the output changes. Flux measurements and perturbations of the shared pool then answer the distinct questions of stationarity and return.</p>
